{"rationale":"1. test_string_bfs | requirement: print the number of distinct strings reachable after zero or more stated operations | technique: direct small-input oracle | oracle: exhaustive BFS count must equal output | validity: BFS includes the initial string and every legal replacement.
2. test_tuple_bfs | requirement: same | technique: direct oracle | oracle: tuple-state exhaustive count | validity: each unequal adjacent pair has exactly one third character.
3. test_layered_closure | requirement: same | technique: direct oracle | oracle: repeatedly close reachable-state frontier | validity: closure is precisely zero-or-more operations.
4. test_recursive_closure | requirement: same | technique: direct oracle | oracle: recursively union all legal descendants | validity: string length decreases on every operation.
5. test_numeric_bfs | requirement: same | technique: direct oracle | oracle: base-3 character encoding BFS | validity: 3-a-b is the unique third symbol.
6. test_list_queue | requirement: same | technique: direct oracle | oracle: queue enumeration | validity: it applies every specified move.
7. test_set_frontier | requirement: same | technique: direct oracle | oracle: fixed-point reachable set | validity: finite decreasing-length state graph.
8. test_explicit_third | requirement: same | technique: direct oracle | oracle: explicit ABC filtering | validity: it directly implements the rule.
9. test_predecessor_free | requirement: same | technique: direct oracle | oracle: forward enumeration only | validity: no unsupported reverse-operation assumption.
10. test_memoized_descendants | requirement: same | technique: direct oracle | oracle: cached descendant union | validity: all paths terminate. I would drop test_predecessor_free first because it is most redundant.","tests":[{"name":"test_string_bfs","source":"def test_string_bfs(run, x):
    out = run(x).strip()
    n, s = x.split()
    n = int(n)
    if n > 8:
        return
    seen = {s}
    todo = [s]
    while todo:
        t = todo.pop()
        for i in range(len(t) - 1):
            if t[i] != t[i + 1]:
                z = ({'A', 'B', 'C'} - {t[i], t[i + 1]}).pop()
                u = t[:i] + z + t[i + 2:]
                if u not in seen:
                    seen.add(u)
                    todo.append(u)
    assert out.isdecimal()
    assert int(out) == len(seen) % 1000000007
"},{"name":"test_tuple_bfs","source":"def test_tuple_bfs(run, x):
    out = run(x).strip()
    parts = x.split()
    n = int(parts[0])
    if n > 8:
        return
    start = tuple(parts[1])
    seen = {start}
    todo = [start]
    alphabet = ('A', 'B', 'C')
    while todo:
        t = todo.pop()
